WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:09.809
The sports world is full of accomplished
competitors. In many cases, it's a family

00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:15.809
affair. Two-time NBA MVP Steph Curry, and
reigning NFL MVP Patrick Mahomes, had

00:00:15.809 --> 00:00:19.380
dads who played pro ball. The father of
iconic New England Patriots coach Bill

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:23.519
Belichick literally wrote the book on
coaching. Indeed, having a parent who was

00:00:23.519 --> 00:00:27.930
a professional athlete turns out to be a
huge leg up when it comes to pursuing

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:32.430
this glamorous, lucrative line of work.
How should we feel about this? And how

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:36.090
should it inform our thoughts about
educational equity? On the one hand, it's

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:40.860
patently unfair. Some children get a huge
head start. On the other hand, parents

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:45.059
will inevitably share passions, knowledge,
and natural gifts with their children. In

00:00:45.059 --> 00:00:49.230
fact, doing so is pretty much the
definition of good parenting. There are a

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:53.039
host of troubling ways in which
advantage is reproduced, but there are

00:00:53.039 --> 00:00:57.690
also organic, socially beneficial ways in
which responsible parents may

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:01.920
incidentally advantage their children,
and well-meaning reformers committed to

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.460
educational equity need to respect that
complicated reality.

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:07.800
What lessons do you

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:12.060
think sports can teach about equity and
advantage? Let us know in the comments.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:16.770
Also, let us know what other topics you'd
like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds,

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:23.420
and be sure to like and subscribe for
more research and videos from AEI.

